{-
   Fibers, and the materialized iterators built on them.

   Module `Fiber`. Under its own name rather than under `Native` because what is here is written in
   the language: the two machine operations it stands on are `Native.stackInit` and
   `Native.stackSwitch`, and everything else - who owns the stack, what a transfer word means, when a
   context is finished - is ordinary Yana over them.

   § What a fiber is here

   A `Fiber` is two saved stack pointers and a status. One of them names the context while the
   consumer is running and the other names the consumer while the context is running, and exactly one
   of the two is live at any moment. That is the whole runtime shape - there is no scheduler, no
   queue and no thread. `doc/inflight/Design-Memory-Revised.md` §17 is the reason it stops there:
   an internal `iter fn`, a materialized iterator and a task fiber share this substrate and differ in
   contract, and a substrate that had picked one of the three would not serve the other two.

   § What is not checked

   Everything the compiler would have to see the two stacks at once to check. In particular:

    - **A context must be finished before its `Fiber` dies.** `finishOn` is what ends one, and it
      does not return. A `Fiber` dropped while its context is suspended leaks whatever that context
      owned, and reusing its stack afterwards runs a live frame's storage as fresh bytes.

    - **A suspended context's locals are not visible to the ownership passes.** They are on a stack
      the compiler was never told about, so a value moved into a context is out of its reach until
      the context hands it back.

    - **The stack is not bounded.** Nothing here measures how much a context can use and nothing
      guards the low end of the mapping. `doc/inflight/Analysis-External-Iterators.md` §4.4 and §4.5
      are the plan: the resolver already publishes whether a call graph is finite
      (`Summary::stack`), and the byte count is the backend's half and is not built. Until it is, a
      stack size is a number the author picks and is the same promise Tritium's `LocalStack` asks
      for.

   Those are the reasons `iter(...)` in a result type - the source form §2.1 proposes, with its
   handle rooted in the creating frame and its loans checked - is a language feature and this is not
   it. This is what that feature will be built out of, usable now.
-}

import Native

{-
   Where a context is, from outside it.

   Three states and not five: `Running` is not one of them, because a `Fiber` is only ever *read* by
   a side that is running, and the side that is running is the one that is not suspended. A field
   saying which would be a fact each side could only ever read about the other.
-}
pub data qualified FiberStatus = Ready | Suspended | Finished

pub data Fiber {
    -- Where the context is suspended. Written by the context itself, every time it hands control
    -- back, and read by `resume`.
    child: %U8,

    -- And where this side is suspended while the context runs. The mirror of the field above: this
    -- side writes it on the way out and the context reads it on the way back.
    parent: %U8,

    status: FiberStatus
}

{-
   `newFiber(stack, stackBytes, context, entry)` - a context prepared, not yet run.

   `stack` is the low address of the bytes the context is to run on and `stackBytes` how many; the
   top is rounded down to the sixteen-byte boundary `stackInit` requires, so a page-aligned mapping
   needs no arithmetic from the caller. Nothing here owns those bytes: they must outlive the context
   and giving them back is the caller's, which is what `Native.Thread` asks for one level down.

   `entry` is handed `context` when the first `resume` arrives, and **must not return** - see
   `finishOn`. A closure is admitted, so the record a context needs may be captured rather than
   pointed at; `context` is a pointer because the usual thing to hand a context is the record holding
   its own `Fiber`, and that record has to be at an address both sides agree on.
-}
pub fn (a) newFiber(stack: %U8, stackBytes: Size, context: %a, entry: (%a) -> {}) -> Fiber:
    -- Down to sixteen rather than up: rounding up would put the first words of the context above
    -- the storage the caller owns.
    let raw = bitcast(stack + stackBytes) :: Size
    let top = bitcast(raw `and` not(15 :: Size)) :: %U8

    return Fiber {
        child: stackInit(top, context, entry),
        parent: null(),
        status: FiberStatus.Ready
    }

{-
   `resume(fiber, value)` - control, given to the context.

   Answers the word the context sends back, which is whatever it passed to `suspend` or to `finish`.
   Returns immediately with zero if the context has already finished, so a driver loop may ask
   without checking first.

   The two reads either side of the switch are what the operation *is*: `self.child` is where the
   context is, and `addressOf(self.parent)` is where it is to find this side when it wants to come
   back. The compiler places both in registers before the stack pointer moves, which is why this is
   an ordinary expression rather than something that has to be written in assembly.
-}
pub fn resume(&self: Fiber, value: Size) -> Size:
    if self.status == FiberStatus.Finished then return 0

    self.status = FiberStatus.Suspended
    return stackSwitch(self.child, addressOf(self.parent), value)

{-
   `suspendOn(fiber, value)` - control, given back, from inside the context.

   The mirror of `resume`, and the same two fields the other way round. Answers the word the next
   `resume` supplies.

   **Through a pointer rather than a borrow, unlike `resume` beside it**, and the asymmetry is
   deliberate: the consumer holds the `Fiber` as ordinary storage of its own, while the context
   reaches it through the address `stackInit` handed over, and a context's body is where that address
   arrives. So the borrow is taken here rather than asked of the caller, and the caller writes
   `suspendOn(self, ...)` with the pointer it was given.
-}
pub fn suspendOn(self: %Fiber, value: Size) -> Size:
    let &me = borrowMut(self)
    return stackSwitch(me.parent, addressOf(me.child), value)

{-
   `finishOn(fiber, value)` - the last hand-over, which does not come back.

   A context ends by switching away rather than by returning, because there is no frame under its
   entry to return into: `stackInit` prepares a stack whose bottom is a trap. So the shape of every
   fiber body is work, hand-overs, and this - and the loop below is what "does not come back" is
   spelled as in a language with no bottom type. A second `resume` of a finished context answers zero
   without switching, so the loop runs at most once more than the program asks for.
-}
pub fn finishOn(self: %Fiber, value: Size):
    let &me = borrowMut(self)
    me.status = FiberStatus.Finished

    while True:
        stackSwitch(me.parent, addressOf(me.child), value)

-- Whether the context has run to its end. A `Fiber` that answers `True` owns nothing and its stack
-- may be given back.
pub fn hasFinished(self: Fiber) -> Bool = self.status == FiberStatus.Finished
